1.28.m:
Homage be to Vāmadeva, Mahādeva, the creator,
to Śambhu, Sthāṇu, ever to Śiva, Parameṣṭhin;
homage to Soma, Rudra, the Great Devourer, the cause.

1.29.m:
Jaimini said:
Lord, you ought truly to cut away this single doubt;
for there is nothing unknown to you, the supreme seer.

The Lord said:
“This higher knowledge cannot be spoken, this knowledge rejected by the ignorant;
I shall tell you according to truth that which has been declared by the supreme seers.

Freed from birth, death, and old age, he goes to the supreme abode of Śiva;
for that is the destination of those who desire liberation, never to die again,
attaining which he has achieved his purpose—so the learned consider.”

1.31.m:
Siddhas and hosts of gods in the sky sang his praises;
charming divine apsarases danced;
Gandharvas, Vidyādharas, Kiṃnaras, and others
showered a rain of flowers mingled with water.

I go constantly for refuge to the lord of Guha;
I resort to Sthāṇu, Giriśa, the enemy of Tripura;
I resort to Śiva, Hara, who has the moon upon his crown,
to you, the wielder of the bow Pināka, I go for refuge.”

Then Śaṅkukarṇa, his self liberated, identical with that, all-pervading, spotless,
merged into the pure liṅga;
it was like a miracle.

1.35.m:
He dwells in a dreadful hell for a myriad hundred kalpas;
therefrom turns [away no one, for] dreadful indeed is the anger of cattle;
and the ancestors of that embodied soul do not accept water [offered by him].

headed by Sanatkumāra, and other sage-brahmins,
serpent-deities, bird-deities, and Siddhas, continually dwell;
and Lord Hari resides there, honored by the Lord of Creatures.
